//? Title: Mood median, Kruskal–Wallis and van der Waerden tests
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Simulate three independent groups with a common spread and separated
locations. All three tests use the observed groups, with different reductions
of the data. A separate rank-sum comparison contrasts the first and last
groups as a specified comparison.
)

> Simulate, test and plot

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/independent_groups.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace sp = dm::stats;
using dm::graphics::RPlot;

int main() {
    dm::set_seed(27);
    const std::vector<std::vector<double>> groups{
        dm::rnorm(0, 1, 28).values(), dm::rnorm(0.45, 1, 28).values(), dm::rnorm(0.9, 1, 28).values()
    };
    const auto median = sp::median_test(groups);
    const auto kw = sp::kruskal_wallis_test(groups);
    const auto scores = sp::van_der_waerden_test(groups);
    const auto rank_sum = sp::wilcoxon_rank_sum_test(groups.front(), groups.back());
    std::print("| Method | Statistic | p-value |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto* test : {&median, &kw, &scores, &rank_sum})
        std::print("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.4g} |\n", test->method, test->statistic, test->p_value);

    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    RPlot::boxplot(groups, {"A", "B", "C"}).abline_h(median.estimate1)
        .title("Three independent groups").x_label("Group").y_label("Response")
        .save_svg("build/independent_groups.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Method | Statistic | p-value |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Mood median test (Pearson chi-square) | 0.286 | 0.8669 |
| Kruskal-Wallis rank sum test | 4.161 | 0.1249 |
| van der Waerden normal scores test | 4.857 | 0.08817 |
| Wilcoxon rank sum test with continuity correction | 271.000 | 0.04831 |
\image(figures/independent_groups.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Group boxplots with a horizontal line at the pooled median used by Mood’s test.)
\alttext(Group boxplots with a horizontal line at the pooled median used by Mood’s test.)

> Interpretation

Mood's test keeps only the side of the pooled median and discards any
observations exactly on it. Kruskal–Wallis keeps pooled ranks, and van der
Waerden uses Normal quantiles of those ranks. Similar distributional shapes
support the location-shift interpretation in this simulation. The prespecified
rank-sum comparison uses only groups A and C.

> References

- [NIST van der Waerden normal scores test|https://itl.nist.gov/div898/software/dataplot/refman1/auxillar/vanderwa.htm]
- [R Kruskal–Wallis test|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/kruskal.test.html]
- [SciPy Mood median test|https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.median_test.html]
